 In this work, we study this question motivated by the complex structure in self-attention heads and the lack of understanding of how kernel self-attentions integrate information from a query sequence of variable length (Q), key sequence (K) of variable length, and value sequence (V). We first reveal a new synchronization mechanism in self-attention's dot-product key and dot-product value modules through experiments. This synchronization mechanism makes the key and value, even with varying lengths, to have similar properties. We then inject a controlled spatial image perturbation into these keys and values, and find a scale-invariant spatial pooling mechanism that is due to the ReLU activation function in attention kernels, which actually averages "sufficiently large" spatial features of the query. We hereafter call this weighted averaging mechanism as the Spatial Attention Behaviour. Later, we find that specific multiplicative factors in the attention "AM, TW, and additive terms AV apply further modulations on this Spatial Attention mechanism, which enables the attention to leverage the combination of absolute and relative positions of the image patches, while the AD term mainly controls stop CNN attention weight computation from saturation. At last, incorporating contextual information happens in the bidirectional causal context provided by the window rank attention mechanism termed as Rank-Attention Behaviour. We dig deeper into the Rank-Attention Behaviour by experiments and find that position embeddings play as both input and output in the dot-product modules of the key and value. Through the Rank-Attention Behaviour, the attention model can aggregate the feature context containing all possible positions into its self-attention kernel. This prefeaturizing significantly removes the dependency on the input sequence to be sorted based on its position. Finally, based on our understanding of the attentions, we conduct dynamic suppression experiments in vision transformers and find that by controlling our spatio-temporal multi-head attention behavior, significant speed-up can be achieved, without any performance degradation. If you are interested in our work, please read the paper! [Link](https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04005)
