In PyTorch, floating-point tensors are represented as arrays in the Python programming language. Each element in the array corresponds to a value in the tensor, and the shape of the array determines the shape of the tensor. For example, a 2x3 floating-point tensor would be represented as a 2x3 array of floating-point numbers in Python.

The specific data type of the floating-point tensor can be specified using the dtype attribute. By default, PyTorch uses the float32 data type, but other data types such as float64 (double precision) can also be used.

Here is an example of how to create a floating-point tensor in PyTorch:
