[70/200] Running: What are tensor parallel and tensor slicing?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Both operations allow you to use efficient techniques to break up variable sized tensors in high dimensions, and there are obvious connections between the two. This post, adapted from a lecture I gave in the Spring 2020 Differential Geometry and Regenerative Computing class, will discuss both operations and their applications to machine learning and Bernard Riemann’s geometric intuitions.

## Diagrammatic Representation

The diagrammatic representation comic above is intended to help visualize the difference between tensor slicing and tensor parallel. The four resulting tensors after a slice on $a$ are stacked on top of one another; almost an arbitrary number of “traces” could be taken along an axis to obtain a vector of the traces. In contrast, the result of a parallel operation on $a$ at “levels” $i$ through $j$ would be a sum along that “slice” -- this is fully characterized as a single element of a vector of the same rank as $a$.

To highlight the parallel case, we’re restricting ourselves to parallel operations on contiguous slices of $a$ (counted as steps in the paralleloid). This make efficient use of matrix multiplication and other low rank approximations. Unless otherwise stated, we’ll assume we’re running some operation on a snippet from a tensor, $a$, and operating on it to parallelize as a vector $p$ with respect to some axis $\texttt{axis}$. At a high level, a variety of techniques will allow us to either construct an efficient parallelizable tensor from $a$, or calculate an efficient formula for that parallel vector $p$.

## Tensor Slicing

The notion of tensor slicing is more of a conceptual exercise than a practical one -- remember, we’re breaking a higher dimensional tensor down into a lower-dimensional tensor, and that’s pretty much all there is to it. Tensor slicing can be especially useful when you need to perform a single operation with varying numbers of tensors with the same number of shapes, and to avoid needing to either broadcast those values to a larger tensor or pad the smaller one to match the shape of larger tensor. Deep residual networks for instance, make use of tensor operations that are applied to varying numbers of variable sized tensors. In these cases, tensor slicing comes to the rescue by allowing us to use pracetical matrix operations to implement those variable dimension operations.^{1}

The equation for tensor slicing is well known:

Given a tensor $a\in \mathbb{R}^{i\times j\times k\cdots}$, borrowing some notations from grid and mesh transformations, we can e. g. slice along an axis:

[math] p_n \in \mathbb{R}^k \quad a_{:,:,n} [/math]

While we’re mainly focusing this post on lower rank counterparts for tensors -- vectors for rank-2, matrices for rank-3, $\cdots$, how do we justify slicing general R-rank tensors? Well, we don’t either need an external Tensorflow API or Python operations to handle variable shape tensors, we simply call those value indexing in accountably mathematical terms! Tensor slicing is referring to the act indexing into a higher dimensional array or tensor, going down a hierarchy of dimensions, placeholderly denoted $a_{i_1,i_2,\dots,i_R}$, index selection $i_x$, and assemble appropriate entries forming a lower rank tensor. In this case, $R$ is fixed with a simply variable number of entries, $N$. The tensor slice corresponds to fixing one of the indices to get a lower rank tensor. The natural sequence of this process would be starting with an massive rank-R tensor, reducing one rank at a time with incrementing upper R indices, which corresponds to rows, columns, … In this case, the tensor derived from slicing on $R-1$ indices would be $O(N^2)$ in rank R space.

In the case of matrices $a\in \mathbb{R}^{3N\times 3N}$, slicing the second outer dimension will result in taking $R=2, N=3^2$ matrices $\in \mathbb{R}^{3N\times 3N}$. In this exceptional case of a rank 2, R-operators formed by a single contraction can be represented as 3D array. If we denote those tensor-like entities produced by tensor slicing as $p_{:,x}$, we have obtained $R-1$ intermediate uncompressed matrices, which by themselves already haven’t any geometric informations for us as they are really matrices
