 We conduct comprehensive experiments on classification tasks over three iconic datasets, bAbI story, Wiki-SQL, and ARC.
We show that token alignment does significantly improve the performance of downstream tasks.
A series of ablation studies suggest that the impact of token alignment mainly depends on the types of tokens.
Certain types of tokens are closely related, and they directly imply semantic information.
Such tokens will improve downstream tasks through token alignment.
On the other hand, other types such as structural tokens convey less meaningful information.
The improvement in downstream performance resulting from the token alignment of these tokens is minor.
We find that the performance differences between the ~\texttt{word-based} and ~\texttt{token-based} models with alignment, even if small, still positively correlate with the semantic relatedness of the tokens being aligned, suggesting alignment can augment semantic relations implicit in the token embeddings.
