 As per the paper "Generative Adversarial Textual Inversion" (https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13486), the authors propose a method that inverts generated text back to images using Attention scores.

The attention scores are computed using the attention mechanism in a Transformer model. In the Transformer model, the self-attention mechanism calculates the attention scores by multiplying the query (Q), key (K), and value (V) vectors as follows:

Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QK^T)V

Here, Q, K, and V are derived from the input text sequence and weighted sum of these derived vectors is used to generate the attention scores.

The attention scores represent the importance or relevance of each word in the input text sequence to other words in the sequence. The idea is that if an image generates relevant words in the text sequence, then the attention scores between these words should be high. By assessing the attention scores between each pair of words, the authors in the above-mentioned paper propose a method to recover the visually relevant pixels in the input image.
[108/200] Running: What are residual connections in transformers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Residual connections in transformers are a technique used to alleviate the vanishing gradient problem, particularly in deep networks. In the case of Transformer models, these connections are referred to as "skip connections" or "shortcut connections".

The main idea behind residual connections is to seamlessly connect the input to the output of a layer, allowing the gradients to pass through more easily. A Residual Transformer, or Transformer with residual connections, consists of a "Residual Block" or "Residual Layer".

Here's a basic representation of a Residual Block in a Transformer architecture:

    Encoder or Decoder Layer
        Attention mechanism (Multi-head self-attention)
        Feed-forward neural network
    + Input
    = Output

The '+' symbol represents the addition of the input to the output of the current Residual Block. This not only helps the gradients to flow through the network more efficiently but also allows the model to learn more complex representations without increasing the depth of the network too much.
