In deep learning, dropout is a technique used to prevent overfitting. During training, the idea is to randomly set a fraction of neurons in a neural network to 0 (i.e., they don't "participate" in processing) at each training iteration. This simulates the scenario where some neurons might not be functioning or available during inference at test time. As a result, each neuron learns to function independently and doesn't solely depend on other neurons for learning the mammography images, which helps generalize better during inference. It also introduces a form of regularization during training to minimize overfitting. It's important to note that we remove the dropout during evaluation or inference to allow all the neurons to participate and to give each neuron a "fair chance" to contribute to the final output.
