[139/200] Running: What are attention heads and how do they specialize?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In transformer models, self-attention allows the model to focus on specific parts of the input sequence when making predictions for other parts. However, self-attention can be computationally expensive, especially for long sequences. Attention heads are a way to address this problem by applying self-attention in parallel across multiple streams, each with reduced computational complexity.

Each attention head is a separate stream that computes attention on its own, using its own weights. The idea is that by separating the attention mechanism into multiple heads, each head can specialize in attending to different types of patterns in the input sequence. For example, one head might attend to the important words, while another head might attend to the structural relationships between the words in the sentence. By aggregating the outputs of all the attention heads, the transformer model can obtain a more complete and nuanced understanding of the input sequence.

In practice, the number of attention heads is a hyperparameter that can be tuned based on the complexity of the task at hand. More attention heads can lead to better performance, but at the cost of increased computation and memory requirements. It is also possible to use different numbers of attention heads for different layers of the transformer.

In addition to being used in self-attention, attention heads can also be used in cross-attention, where the model attends to input tokens based on query tokens generated from another sequence. In this case, the attention heads can help the model focus on the relevant parts of the query sequence when making predictions for the input sequence.

Overall, attention heads are a powerful tool for building transformer models that can effectively handle complex sequential data. By dividing the attention mechanism into multiple streams, attention heads allow the model to specialize and better capture the underlying patterns in the data.
